
  I'll brainstorm [1m#1, the tasks core[0m, now. But one question upstream of it shapes everything, including whether #2 and
  #3 are even real.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this is a single-person tool or a shared one. It decides the whole stack, and it's the expensive
  thing to change later.

  - [1mSingle-user, browser-only[0m — tasks in [38;5;153mlocalStorage[39m, no server, no accounts, deploys as static files. Cheapest by far.
    But it makes notifications near-meaningless: if you're the only one who changes anything, there's nothing to be
    told. "Notifications" would collapse into due-date reminders.
  - [1mMulti-user, server-backed[0m — real accounts, a database, an API. This is what your original request actually implies:
    other people change tasks, so you need telling. Much more to build, and it's the choice you can't reverse cheaply —
    retrofitting auth and a server onto a localStorage app means rewriting the data layer and every component that
    touches it.
  - [1mSingle-user now, server later[0m — build local-only but keep storage behind an interface. Sounds prudent; in practice
    the seam rarely survives contact with real auth, and you pay for the abstraction while getting none of its benefit.

  [1mMy recommendation: multi-user, server-backed.[0m You opened by asking for notifications, and notifications are a
  multi-actor feature. Building the cheap version first would mean throwing it away the moment the actual goal arrives.
